AbstractWind is a fundamental driver of land–atmosphere exchange, yet its role in ecosystem water use efficiency (WUE) remains unclear. Here using global eddy-covariance observations combined with Earth system model simulations, we show that WUE declines with increasing wind speed across most terrestrial ecosystems, driven primarily by wind-induced increases in evapotranspiration and secondarily by reductions in gross primary productivity. Mechanistically, stronger winds enhance aerodynamic conductance, strengthen ecosystem–atmosphere coupling and amplify atmospheric water demand. Earth system model simulations capture the negative sensitivity of WUE to wind speed and indicate that future WUE changes will be strongly influenced by wind speed. Under high-emission scenarios, intensified reductions in wind speed enhance WUE, whereas weaker stilling under low-emission scenarios reduces this effect. These results establish wind speed as a previously underappreciated regulator of carbon–water coupling and highlight its importance for predicting ecosystem responses to climate change.

